          "issue": "The selected Greek supports “the devil’s daughter,” but the note presents specific external attestations and their locations as established facts without supplying extracts or identifying them as unverified editorial reports. The supplied material does not establish those external readings. This is a provenance-disclosure defect, not evidence that the reported alternatives are false.",
          "suggested_revision": "Retain “the devil’s daughter.” Supply bounded source extracts and provenance supporting the external attestations, or explicitly attribute them to an editorial report and disclose that their wording and locations are not verified by the material supplied for this review. Any replacement note requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "Πάντων προέχουσα ἐπιθυμία γυναικὸς ἀλλοτρίας ἢ ἀνδρός",
          "english_quote": "Another’s wife or husband preserves both sexes and the shared other-person construction. The Greek does not restrict this clause to a man desiring a wife.",
          "issue": "This note is classified as “textual_variant,” although it explains the selected wording rather than identifying a different source reading. Notes c and d have the same classification problem: c supplies English interpretive alternatives, and d explains the preserved Lord/God distinction. This misrepresents translation explanations as textual-variant evidence.",
          "suggested_revision": "Classify notes a, c, and d as translation, lexical, or source-wording explanations using the supported schema. Reserve “textual_variant” for a genuinely different reported source reading. Their explanatory wording need not be replaced merely to correct the classification.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
